// ── Comprehension passages (original texts written for VIBHA) ─────────────
const RC1 =
  "Every generation of teachers believes its students are more distracted than the last, but the present complaint has a sharper edge. The devices that carry our lessons also carry an endless supply of alternatives to them. A notification is not merely an interruption; it is a small wager placed on our curiosity, and the house usually wins. Research on task-switching suggests that what feels like multitasking is really rapid alternation, and each switch carries a cost in time and accuracy that the switcher rarely notices. Students who keep messaging apps open during study sessions tend to report that they learned as much as usual, while their test performance tells a different story.\n\nYet banning devices is a blunt remedy. The same phone that distracts can also hold a dictionary, a library and a tutor. The more useful question is not whether technology should be present but who is directing attention: the learner or the designer of the app. Teachers can help by making the purpose of each task explicit, by building short periods of uninterrupted work into lessons, and by teaching students to notice when their attention has drifted. Attention, on this view, is less a fixed trait than a skill, and like other skills it improves with deliberate practice and deteriorates with neglect.";

const RC2 =
  "Histories of mathematics written in the twentieth century often moved directly from the Greeks to the European Renaissance, as though nothing of consequence happened in between. Scholars working on Sanskrit and Malayalam manuscripts have complicated that story. Between the fourteenth and sixteenth centuries, a line of astronomer-mathematicians in Kerala, beginning with Madhava of Sangamagrama, developed infinite series for trigonometric functions and for π, results that would appear in Europe only centuries later. Their work was written in verse, transmitted from teacher to student, and justified by careful demonstrations, called yukti, rather than by the axiomatic proofs familiar from Euclid.\n\nThe discovery raises a difficult question about what counts as 'mathematics'. If proof must take the Greek form, the Kerala results look like clever calculation. If proof means a convincing, shareable justification, they are mathematics in the full sense. Some historians caution against a reverse error: claiming every modern idea was 'already known' in ancient texts. That habit replaces one myth with another and makes careful historical work harder. The most defensible position is that knowledge has grown in many places, in many styles of reasoning, and that recognising this enriches rather than diminishes the achievements of any single tradition.";

const RC3 =
  "India is the world's largest user of groundwater, drawing more than the United States and China combined. Tube wells transformed agriculture after the 1970s: a farmer no longer had to wait for the canal or the monsoon. But the same freedom created a classic collective-action problem. Each farmer gains by pumping a little more, while the cost of a falling water table is shared by everyone, including those not yet born. Subsidised or free electricity for pumping, introduced for good reasons, removed the main signal that might have encouraged restraint.\n\nTechnical fixes alone have rarely solved the problem. Check dams and recharge wells help where aquifers can absorb water, but in hard-rock regions storage is limited. Programmes that have worked combine information with collective rules: villages measure their own water budgets, agree on crop choices, and monitor one another. Such arrangements succeed when people trust the data and see benefits within a few seasons. They fail when outside agencies impose them without local ownership. The lesson is that groundwater is not only a hydrological resource but a social one, governed by institutions as much as by rainfall.";

const RC4 =
  "John Henry Newman, writing in the 1850s, imagined the university as a place for the cultivation of the intellect for its own sake, where students would acquire a 'philosophical habit of mind' through contact with many disciplines. Half a century earlier in Berlin, Wilhelm von Humboldt had proposed a different emphasis: the unity of teaching and research, in which professors and students together pursued new knowledge in freedom. Modern universities have inherited both ideals and added a third, often louder demand: that higher education should prepare graduates for employment and contribute to economic growth.\n\nThese aims need not conflict, but they compete for time, money and prestige. A curriculum built entirely around current job titles risks becoming obsolete as quickly as the titles do. A curriculum indifferent to work risks failing students who borrowed to attend. The more durable answer may be to treat broad intellectual capacities, such as reasoning, writing, quantitative literacy and ethical judgment, as the most practical preparation of all, because they transfer across changing occupations. On this view, the old liberal ideal and the new vocational demand meet in the same place: an education that teaches people how to keep learning.";

const RC5 =
  "Science is often described as self-correcting, but correction is not automatic. It depends on people repeating experiments, publishing the results, and being rewarded for doing so. In the past decade, large collaborative projects tried to reproduce well-known findings in psychology and other fields. A substantial proportion of the effects they examined were weaker than originally reported, and some could not be reproduced at all. The causes were mundane rather than sinister: small samples, flexible analysis choices, and journals that preferred surprising positive results to careful null findings.\n\nThe response has been equally mundane. Researchers increasingly register their hypotheses and analysis plans before collecting data, share materials and data openly, and run larger studies. Some journals now accept papers on the basis of the question and method, before the results are known, so that publication does not depend on whether the effect 'worked'. None of these practices guarantees truth. They simply make it harder to fool ourselves, which, as the physicist Richard Feynman observed, is the first principle of science, since we are the easiest people to fool.";

const RC6 =
  "When women in a village form a self-help group, the first visible change is financial: members save small amounts weekly and lend to one another at agreed rates. Banks, which once saw such households as too poor to serve, began lending to the groups because the members guaranteed one another's repayment. Repayment rates have often been high, and many governments have built national programmes on this model.\n\nThe less visible changes may matter more. Regular meetings give women a public role that was previously unavailable to them; some members later stand for panchayat elections. At the same time, critics warn against romanticising the model. Credit can deepen debt when the income it is meant to generate does not materialise, and group pressure to repay can become coercive. Evaluations that compare groups with similar non-member households find modest average gains in income but larger gains in women's mobility and say in household decisions. The evidence suggests that self-help groups are better understood as institutions for building social capital than as a quick route out of poverty.";

